 ## Background
-The standard NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. [1] in 2020, utilizing hierarchical rendering with coarse and fine networks to improve computational efficiency. Subsequent works have focused on accelerating training and inference, with most efforts falling into architectural modifications such as input expansion, output expansion, or representation substitution. The original implementation used dense layers with ReLU activations, with default parameters including a network depth of 8 layers and 256 channels per layer [S3, unofficial].
+The standard NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1][S3, unofficial]. It established the baseline of using two MLPs (coarse and fine) with positional encoding to map 3D coordinates and view directions to density and color. Subsequent modifications have expanded this foundation to handle dynamic scenes [S5], sparse views [S1], semantic understanding [S1], and real-time rendering [S1][S4, unofficial]. The field has evolved from simple input expansions (like time parameters [S5]) to complex architectural changes like voxel grids [S1][S4, unofficial], SDFs [S1], and hybrid representations [S1].
 
